  <h2>What we check on every Google Business Profile</h2>
  <p>
    We treat Whitespark’s local ranking factors (and Search Atlas’s checklist) as a practical punch
    list. Primary category alone has made or broken lead flow for clients. Other
    high-impact items: proximity/office placement when you have a choice, keywords in the profile
    name, showing a physical address, complete services, NAP consistency on the site, and a steady
    stream of real reviews.
  </p>
  <p>
    It’s a competition game. Most shops never finish the basics. Doing them well is usually enough
    to move, then we keep going on content, links, and area pages where the market demands it.
  </p>

  <h2>How we explain heatmaps to owners</h2>
  <p>
    We run grids in Search Atlas around the times your trade actually gets searched (for many
    electricians, morning hours). Top-three coverage in the neighborhoods you care about means more
    map-pack clicks at a fraction of ad cost. Rankings ebb with competition and reviews, we don’t
    pretend otherwise. We do the controllable work and expand with pages or posts when a pocket of
    the map stays dark.
  </p>

  <h2>Reviews without the spam theater</h2>
  <p>
    Small shops (1-3 techs) win with simple end-of-job habits and CRM reminders. Larger crews need
    the same habit systemized so it doesn’t depend on the owner. We coach for feedback, photos, and
    consistency, not sketchy review farms. Google cares about volume, recency, and quality; owners
    who ask poorly or buy reviews eventually pay for it.
  </p>
